ed the pros and cons of checking versus raising. Considering the opponent's limping behavior and the potential for a larger pot, I'm committed to the aggressive route, opting for a raise to 300 to build value and isolate the opponent.\n\n\n", 'output': 'Evaluation: The hand Ace of Clubs and Queen of Diamonds (AQo) is a premium starter in a heads-up match. It possesses high equity against a random hand (approx. 65%) and significantly dominates an opponent\'s limping range. The opponent\'s action of "0" (Check/Call) indicates a limp from the Small Blind/Button. Checking back (Action 0) would be a passive play, allowing the opponent to see a flop for free with potentially weak cards that could outdraw high cards. Raising isolates the opponent, builds the pot while holding the equity advantage, and takes the initiative, which is vital as we will be playing out of position post-flop.\n\nReason: Raising is the most +EV move to extract value and deny free equity. While a minimum raise (200) is valid, a standard raise size of 3 to 4 big blinds is more effective at folding out trash hands or building a pot against worse value hands. Raising to 300 (3x Big Blind) strikes a balance between aggression and pot control.\n\nAnswer: 300'}
