's best to minimize losses. Bluffing will most likely lose, given my opponent\'s probable range.\n**Answer:** 0\n\n\n', 'output': "Evaluation:\nI hold 6 of Clubs and 4 of Clubs, while the community cards are 10 of Diamonds, Queen of Diamonds, Ace of Hearts, and 9 of Spades. This board texture is very heavy with high cards and connects well with a typical pre-flop raiser's range (straights, pairs of Aces/Queens). My hand has no pair, no straight draw, and no flush draw; it is effectively air with very low showdown value. The opponent raised pre-flop but checked the flop, suggesting they might be exercising pot control or slow-playing, though they could also have missed.\n\nReason:\nSince there is no bet to call currently, checking is the optimal play. It costs nothing and allows me to see the river card for free if the opponent also checks. Betting as a bluff is dangerous because the board (A-Q-10-9) heavily favors the opponent's range, making it likely they have connected with the board in some way and will call. Folding is unnecessary when I can check for free.\n\nAnswer: 0"}
