s. I have no reason to be aggressive here. It's the right play here. I will check (0).\n\n\n", 'output': 'Evaluation: You hold a Pair of 6s, which combined with the board (Ace of Clubs, 6 of Spades, 2 of Diamonds, Ace of Hearts, King of Spades) gives you Two Pair, Aces and Sixes, with a King kicker. The opponent has shown passivity by checking on the Flop, Turn, and River. While your hand has showdown value, it is vulnerable to any King (which makes higher Two Pair), any Ace (Trips), or any pocket pair 77 or higher.\n\nReason: Betting in this spot is known as "betting for thin value," but it is risky here. If you bet, worse hands (like a pair of 2s, pocket 3s-5s, or air) will likely fold, meaning you gain nothing extra. Conversely, better hands (any King, Ace, or higher pocket pair) will call or raise, causing you to lose chips. Checking is the dominant strategy as it allows you to realize your equity at showdown for free, winning the pot against bluffs and weak pairs without risking more chips against a trap or a lucky River card like a King.\n\nAnswer: 0'}
